note as N1
  **Why no do-not-retry flag is needed**

  After step 1 of `_cancel_one_job_inner`
  the DB row is `status='cancelled'`,
  which is in CallbackRouter's
  ``_TERMINAL_GROUP_STATUSES``.

  Any delayed failure signal that
  comes in afterwards (provider's
  monitor's last-tick race, agent's
  /agent-failure callback, backup_monitor
  sweep) hits CallbackRouter.route's
  is_job_terminal short-circuit FIRST
  and gets dropped silently.
  handle_chunk_failed never runs,
  _try_dispatch_retry never runs,
  no retry can fire.

  Step 4's ledger release is
  belt-and-suspenders for the same
  reason: even if a signal somehow
  slipped past the terminal guard,
  release_if_owner would return
  False (row already gone) and
  bail before the retry path.

  No `cancelled_at` column,
  no in-memory set,
  no max-attempts hack.
end note

note as N2
  **Why this isn't "rebuilding the flow"**

  cancel_render today contains
  three `for job in jobs:` blocks
  doing the per-job cancel dance
  (mark cancelled, set available,
  stop monitor, drain, cancel
  provider).

  B2 needs the SAME dance for one
  job from a different caller.

  The work is:
  1. Extract `_cancel_one_job_inner(raw)`
     from cancel_render's loops.
  2. cancel_render calls
     `_cancel_one_job_inner` per job.
  3. New public `cancel_one_job(id)`
     calls the same inner helper +
     reconcile_group_status.
  4. Facade method on
     RenderOrchestrator.
  5. New POST /jobs/{id}/cancel
     route + BackgroundTasks.

  Net new code: ~30 lines.
  Net duplication: zero.
end note

